repository-code: 'https://github.com/lucca-pellegrini/AC3-TP1'
abstract: >-
  Cache hierarchy parameters interact, so their individual
  payoffs are hard to rank. We evaluate five PolyBench
  kernels under gem5, sweeping capacity, line size, and
  associativity one factor at a time from a common baseline,
  so every change is attributable to a single parameter.
  Line size dominates: it cuts L1D misses almost identically
  across kernels, yet buys speedups from 0.5% to 15.4%.
  Associativity is structurally low-leverage for regular
  accesses, and capacity is a threshold effect. One
  quantity, the fraction of execution exposed to L1D miss
  latency, orders the kernels for both factors and predicts
  the line-size speedups via an Amdahl-style model, giving a
  transferable tuning criterion.
keywords:
  - Memory hierarchy
  - Cache memories
  - Simulation
  - Performance analysis and design aids
  - Modeling of computer architecture
